1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Oil. Drillers go bust for it. Nations go to war for it. Nobody can get enough.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:13,000
From a single 50-foot derrick to platform cities taller than the tallest buildings,

3
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:18,000
it's a high-risk gamble that can make you a billionaire or ruin your life.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, passion, luck, and science in the search for black gold on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,460
In the early 1600s, when European explorers first entered the wooded hills of western

6
00:00:41,461 --> 00:00:47,660
Pennsylvania, they found something they'd never encountered before, a black oozing substance

7
00:00:47,661 --> 00:00:52,440
that seeped up from the ground in viscous bubbles.

8
00:00:52,441 --> 00:00:57,160
The local Indians touted the substance as a folk medicine cure-all for everything from

9
00:00:57,161 --> 00:00:59,400
burns to broken bones.

10
00:00:59,401 --> 00:01:03,900
They also used it to caulk their canoes.

11
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,500
Native Americans were not the first to make use

12
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:08,540
of this natural resource.

13
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:10,640
The walls of Jericho were held together

14
00:01:10,740 --> 00:01:13,980
with oil-based tar as mortar.

15
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:17,960
And according to the Bible, Noah applied two coats of tar

16
00:01:18,060 --> 00:01:22,560
to the outside of the ark to make it watertight.

17
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:24,720
Oil had been formed over millions of years

18
00:01:24,820 --> 00:01:28,460
from prehistoric plant and animal life that decayed

19
00:01:28,461 --> 00:01:31,420
and was compressed under great pressure.

20
00:01:31,421 --> 00:01:34,460
But it wasn't until the 1850s that it burst forth

21
00:01:34,461 --> 00:01:37,260
into the modern world and triggered a revolution.

22
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:41,180
The man who launched this revolution was a New York lawyer

23
00:01:41,181 --> 00:01:44,640
with an entrepreneurial flair named George Bissell.

24
00:01:44,641 --> 00:01:47,740
He had seen how Pennsylvania oil was collected

25
00:01:47,741 --> 00:01:50,940
from the surface and used in medicinal elixirs

26
00:01:50,941 --> 00:01:52,380
popular at the time.

27
00:01:53,380 --> 00:01:58,380
Rub it on wounds, cuts, abrasions, rashes, pimples,

28
00:01:58,460 --> 00:02:03,460
sores, scratches, and other afflictions of the epidermis.

29
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:10,080
But Bissell had another idea for this miracle substance.

30
00:02:10,081 --> 00:02:14,620
Could oil, he wondered, be somehow harnessed for illumination?

31
00:02:14,621 --> 00:02:17,580
Lamps burning whale oil were still the dominant source

32
00:02:17,581 --> 00:02:20,060
of home lighting, but the price of whale oil

33
00:02:20,061 --> 00:02:22,680
had been rising precipitously.

34
00:02:22,681 --> 00:02:26,440
Whale oil was extracted from the blubber and oil

35
00:02:26,441 --> 00:02:27,831
the spermaceti whale.

36
00:02:27,832 --> 00:02:32,600
And of course the spermaceti whales were hunted extensively in the 1830s

37
00:02:32,601 --> 00:02:39,320
and 1840s to the point that by the 1850s the world heard as it was accessible at the time

38
00:02:39,321 --> 00:02:40,722
was very much depleted.

39
00:02:40,723 --> 00:02:44,440
And that of course drove the price of spermaceti whale oil as

40
00:02:44,441 --> 00:02:45,975
they called it up.

41
00:02:45,976 --> 00:02:51,860
As a possible alternative to whale oil, Bissell in 1854 sent a sample

42
00:02:51,861 --> 00:02:57,700
of the Pennsylvania oil to a chemistry professor at Yale for analysis.

43
00:02:57,701 --> 00:03:01,820
Three months later, the professor confirmed Bissell's wild dream.

44
00:03:01,821 --> 00:03:07,300
Your company have in their possession a raw material from which, by simple and not expensive

45
00:03:07,301 --> 00:03:12,780
processes, they may manufacture very valuable products.

46
00:03:12,781 --> 00:03:17,120
He discovered that through boiling the oil, it could be distilled into a high-quality

47
00:03:17,121 --> 00:03:22,280
illuminating fluid called kerosene, which burned with a clean, bright flame.

48
00:03:23,140 --> 00:03:28,020
Bissell and his partner, New Haven banker John Townsend, formed the Seneca Oil Company

49
00:03:28,021 --> 00:03:30,380
and acquired land in Pennsylvania.

50
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:35,980
The problem they faced was how to get the oil in large enough quantities to create a

51
00:03:35,981 --> 00:03:37,020
kerosene market.

52
00:03:37,480 --> 00:03:41,100
The solution came from a bottle of make-believe medicine.

53
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:45,180
While in a druggist's shop one day in 1856,

54
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:49,240
Bissell saw an advertisement for a miracle elixir,

55
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:51,120
Keir's Rock Oil Medicine,

56
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:54,440
that featured on its label a derrick used to drill water wells.

57
00:03:55,240 --> 00:03:58,300
Water distillers in Pennsylvania had long been plagued

58
00:03:58,301 --> 00:04:00,660
with the problem of oil seeping into their wells.

59
00:04:01,220 --> 00:04:05,020
It was this unwanted oil that Samuel Keir bottled and sold.

60
00:04:06,020 --> 00:04:08,140
In a giant leap of understanding,

61
00:04:08,141 --> 00:04:12,625
Bissell immediately grasped that drilling for oil might make sense.

62
00:04:12,626 --> 00:04:14,700
What they needed was someone to

63
00:04:14,701 --> 00:04:21,023
supervise the drilling of a well. Townsend suggested his friend Edwin Drake.

64
00:04:21,024 --> 00:04:23,020
Edwin Drake was probably

65
00:04:23,021 --> 00:04:26,969
not the most obvious choice to drill that became the pioneer oil well.

66
00:04:26,970 --> 00:04:28,380
He had been a purser on a

67
00:04:28,381 --> 00:04:32,382
steamboat, he had been a conductor on the New Haven Railroad and so on.

68
00:04:32,383 --> 00:04:33,340
I think his major

69
00:04:33,341 --> 00:04:36,780
qualification for the job was that he needed work at the time.

70
00:04:36,781 --> 00:04:42,780
When it was announced that this well would be drilled for the express purpose of finding

71
00:04:42,781 --> 00:04:48,227
oil in the area there, people, Drake became a laughing stock.

72
00:04:48,228 --> 00:04:50,460
Everybody laughed at him.

73
00:04:51,820 --> 00:04:58,380
So in order for his backers to give him some credibility, all of the mail that went into

74
00:04:58,381 --> 00:05:01,080
had the title Colonel on it.

75
00:05:02,140 --> 00:05:05,100
So when the postmaster thought, oh, he's a colonel.

76
00:05:05,101 --> 00:05:06,760
So he was known as Colonel Drake.

77
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:12,300
In the spring of 1859 in Titusville, Pennsylvania,

78
00:05:12,301 --> 00:05:14,880
Colonel Drake hired a local blacksmith

79
00:05:14,881 --> 00:05:18,980
named Uncle Billy Smith, and they began drilling for oil.

80
00:05:20,860 --> 00:05:24,320
As the months passed, they hit water, but nothing else.

81
00:05:24,321 --> 00:05:27,040
The oil industry's first well seemed destined

82
00:05:27,041 --> 00:05:29,840
to be the oil industry's first dry hole.

83
00:05:30,840 --> 00:05:32,920
As they saw their few thousand dollars

84
00:05:32,921 --> 00:05:34,920
draining down Drake's well,

85
00:05:34,921 --> 00:05:38,080
the investors grew increasingly wary.

86
00:05:38,081 --> 00:05:40,260
Finally, in August 1859,

87
00:05:40,261 --> 00:05:42,660
Townsend sent Drake the last of the money

88
00:05:42,661 --> 00:05:45,960
and told him to close up the operation and return home.

89
00:05:47,020 --> 00:05:51,260
Townsend's letter arrived on Monday, August 29th, 1859,

90
00:05:51,261 --> 00:05:53,760
but to his everlasting gratitude,

91
00:05:53,761 --> 00:05:56,260
it was one day too late,

92
00:05:56,261 --> 00:05:59,580
For on Sunday the 28th, with the well at 69 and 1

93
00:05:59,581 --> 00:06:02,760
half feet, Edwin Drake looked down the hole

94
00:06:02,761 --> 00:06:06,600
and saw something dark floating to the surface.

95
00:06:06,601 --> 00:06:10,260
When he asked what it was, Uncle Billy answered simply,

96
00:06:10,261 --> 00:06:11,720
that's your fortune.

97
00:06:11,721 --> 00:06:14,900
The oil age had just begun.

98
00:06:14,901 --> 00:06:17,900
The well produced 35 barrels of oil a day.

99
00:06:17,901 --> 00:06:22,380
Each barrel held 40 gallons, and they sold it for $40 a barrel,

100
00:06:22,381 --> 00:06:27,580
almost $700 a barrel in today's dollars.

101
00:06:27,581 --> 00:06:31,520
Within days, the first oil boom was underway.

102
00:06:31,521 --> 00:06:35,100
The population of Titusville quadrupled overnight,

103
00:06:35,101 --> 00:06:39,020
and land prices skyrocketed as trainloads of newcomers

104
00:06:39,021 --> 00:06:42,040
converged on the town with money in their hands

105
00:06:42,041 --> 00:06:44,820
and greed in their hearts.

106
00:06:44,821 --> 00:06:48,260
Little more than a year later, Titusville had 75 wells

107
00:06:48,261 --> 00:06:52,700
producing oil and 15 refineries distilling kerosene.

108
00:06:52,701 --> 00:06:55,900
A local newspaper editor wrote of the period,

109
00:06:55,901 --> 00:06:58,040
The oil and land excitement in this section

110
00:06:58,041 --> 00:07:00,500
has already become a sort of epidemic.

111
00:07:00,501 --> 00:07:04,640
It embraces all classes and ages and conditions of men.

112
00:07:04,641 --> 00:07:09,240
They neither talk nor look nor act as they did six months ago.

113
00:07:09,241 --> 00:07:13,180
Land leases, contracts, refusals, deed arrangements,

114
00:07:13,181 --> 00:07:17,880
interests, and all that sort of talk is all they can comprehend.

115
00:07:17,881 --> 00:07:21,380
Among those who got rich were investors George Bissell

116
00:07:21,381 --> 00:07:22,900
and John Townsend.

117
00:07:22,901 --> 00:07:26,960
Among those who didn't was Colonel Edwin Drake.

118
00:07:26,961 --> 00:07:29,940
A poor businessman, Drake did not patent any

119
00:07:29,941 --> 00:07:33,140
of his inventions, like the sinking of a stovepipe

120
00:07:33,141 --> 00:07:35,480
down the well to hold water out and keep the well

121
00:07:35,481 --> 00:07:37,160
from collapsing.

122
00:07:37,161 --> 00:07:40,480
What money he did make, he lost in bad investments

123
00:07:40,481 --> 00:07:42,960
and died a virtual ward of the state,

124
00:07:42,961 --> 00:07:46,080
bitter about the fortunes others had amassed

125
00:07:46,081 --> 00:07:48,420
from his discovery.

126
00:07:48,421 --> 00:07:50,460
It was written of him when he died.

127
00:07:50,461 --> 00:07:55,160
He shook the boughs for others to gather the fruits.

128
00:07:55,161 --> 00:07:59,120
Drake's discovery in Western Pennsylvania lit up the globe.

129
00:07:59,121 --> 00:08:01,000
Today in the 20th century, it's hard

130
00:08:01,001 --> 00:08:04,000
to imagine the impact kerosene could have on the world

131
00:08:04,001 --> 00:08:05,660
of the 19th century.

132
00:08:05,661 --> 00:08:08,040
But it revolutionized society.

133
00:08:08,041 --> 00:08:10,380
As large amounts of oil were pumped up

134
00:08:10,381 --> 00:08:13,300
from the underground reservoir Drake had tapped,

135
00:08:13,301 --> 00:08:20,000
Virtually overnight, kerosene became the dominant source of home illumination in America and Europe.

136
00:08:20,060 --> 00:08:23,560
You probably bought it from a street vendor if you lived in a city.

137
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:27,920
If you lived in a small town, as a lot of Americans still did,

138
00:08:27,921 --> 00:08:31,420
you almost certainly bought kerosene at a general store.

139
00:08:32,260 --> 00:08:39,440
Compared to $5 a gallon for whale oil, kerosene prices varied from 10 to 25 cents a gallon.

140
00:08:39,441 --> 00:08:50,540
From Drake's 35 barrels a day in 1859, the Pennsylvania oil region was only three years later shipping out more than 8,000 barrels of crude oil a day.

141
00:08:51,440 --> 00:08:56,480
Huge refineries opened up in major cities along the rail lines where the oil was sent.

142
00:08:57,060 --> 00:09:01,240
By 1865, Cleveland alone had 30 refineries.

143
00:09:01,960 --> 00:09:09,140
Like the rest of the burgeoning young oil industry, refining, too, was a rough-and-tumble, cutthroat business.

144
00:09:09,440 --> 00:09:16,600
And no one was more perfectly suited to it than a young Cleveland businessman who owned the city's largest refining plant.

145
00:09:17,600 --> 00:09:18,829
His name was John D.

146
00:09:18,830 --> 00:09:26,080
Rockefeller, and his dark genius for the oil business would soon make him the richest and most reviled man in America.

147
00:09:27,160 --> 00:09:27,860
John D.

148
00:09:27,861 --> 00:09:34,820
Rockefeller got into the oil business with a dry goods wholesaling partner during the Civil War.

149
00:09:34,821 --> 00:09:40,100
got into it in a small way with a fairly conventional refinery,

150
00:09:40,620 --> 00:09:43,620
disposing of most of his goods in the immediate vicinity of Cleveland.

151
00:09:44,340 --> 00:09:48,500
By relentlessly pouring profits back into his business,

152
00:09:48,940 --> 00:09:51,440
Rockefeller expanded to a second refinery,

153
00:09:51,700 --> 00:09:54,280
then to an export business to service Europe.

154
00:09:54,860 --> 00:09:57,940
He bought boats, tanker cars, storage facilities.

155
00:09:58,700 --> 00:10:03,500
Finally, in 1870, he consolidated all his businesses under one name,

156
00:10:03,501 --> 00:10:06,000
the Standard Oil Company.

157
00:10:06,100 --> 00:10:07,980
By that point, Rockefeller already

158
00:10:08,080 --> 00:10:12,000
controlled 1 10th of the entire American refining industry.

159
00:10:12,100 --> 00:10:13,640
But that wasn't enough.

160
00:10:13,740 --> 00:10:17,440
He wanted nothing less than to control it all.

161
00:10:17,540 --> 00:10:20,920
In the 1870s, Rockefeller's pursuit of industry control

162
00:10:21,020 --> 00:10:23,760
set a new standard of ruthlessness.

163
00:10:23,860 --> 00:10:27,560
Bribery, extortion, collusion, and intimidation

164
00:10:27,660 --> 00:10:30,220
were just some of the weapons he used.

165
00:10:30,320 --> 00:10:32,620
Those competitors he couldn't take over.

166
00:10:32,621 --> 00:10:35,020
he drove out of business.

167
00:10:35,120 --> 00:10:38,960
By 1880, Standard Oil controlled 90% of the nation's

168
00:10:39,060 --> 00:10:40,720
refining capacity.

169
00:10:40,820 --> 00:10:46,060
By 1890, John D. Rockefeller was the wealthiest man in America.

170
00:10:46,160 --> 00:10:48,340
He moved to New York, dividing his time

171
00:10:48,440 --> 00:10:50,840
between an opulent Manhattan townhouse

172
00:10:50,940 --> 00:10:54,740
and a vast estate in the country north of the city.

173
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:56,580
But acquiring that level of wealth

174
00:10:56,680 --> 00:10:59,780
had also made Rockefeller one of the most feared and hated

175
00:10:59,880 --> 00:11:01,120
men in the country.

176
00:11:01,121 --> 00:11:05,320
Mothers warn their children to be good, or Rockefeller will get you.

177
00:11:06,600 --> 00:11:09,280
Rockefeller really became a popular target,

178
00:11:09,520 --> 00:11:12,120
largely because he had very little sensitivity

179
00:11:12,121 --> 00:11:16,720
to what we today would call public image or public reputation.

180
00:11:17,360 --> 00:11:22,020
Public opinion was certainly that he was an avaricious, ruthless kind of person.

181
00:11:22,920 --> 00:11:26,160
But as Standard Oil's stranglehold on the industry grew,

182
00:11:26,161 --> 00:11:30,340
so did assaults on Rockefeller's monopolistic practices.

183
00:11:31,120 --> 00:11:38,080
In 1904, immediately after his election, President Teddy Roosevelt launched an investigation of Standard Oil.

184
00:11:38,680 --> 00:11:43,560
Two years later, the government brought suit against the company under antitrust provisions.

185
00:11:44,340 --> 00:11:48,820
Roosevelt called Standard's executives the biggest criminals in the country.

186
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:52,360
The case was in the courts for five years.

187
00:11:52,361 --> 00:11:58,200
But in 1911, while he was hunting big game in Africa, Roosevelt got the good news.

188
00:11:58,201 --> 00:12:02,320
Standard Oil would be dismantled into numerous smaller companies.

189
00:12:02,860 --> 00:12:05,520
Rockefeller had lost his monopoly for good.

190
00:12:13,020 --> 00:12:19,000
The breakup of Standard Oil hardly meant the end of massive fortunes to be made from oil,

191
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:23,840
especially since a market 100 times larger than kerosene was about to emerge.

192
00:12:24,460 --> 00:12:26,520
America was about to go car crazy.

193
00:12:26,521 --> 00:12:29,520
Refining kerosene had always left producers

194
00:12:29,620 --> 00:12:31,920
with a hazardous and unwanted byproduct

195
00:12:32,020 --> 00:12:34,760
that was usually just pumped into pits and burned.

196
00:12:34,860 --> 00:12:37,860
It was called gasoline.

197
00:12:37,960 --> 00:12:40,560
But in garages all over Europe and America,

198
00:12:40,660 --> 00:12:42,440
inventors were tinkering with something

199
00:12:42,540 --> 00:12:45,640
known as the internal combustion engine.

200
00:12:45,740 --> 00:12:48,880
And gasoline turned out to be just the right fuel

201
00:12:48,980 --> 00:12:50,580
to put it on the road.

202
00:12:50,680 --> 00:12:52,940
By the turn of the century, the automobile

203
00:12:53,040 --> 00:12:56,520
was a major sensation, said Frank Phillips, founder

204
00:12:56,521 --> 00:12:58,160
of Phillips Petroleum.

205
00:12:58,260 --> 00:13:00,520
I think people are going to buy quite a parcel

206
00:13:00,620 --> 00:13:03,120
of these little gasoline boogies.

207
00:13:03,220 --> 00:13:05,420
They need gasoline to make them go.

208
00:13:05,520 --> 00:13:08,440
It may be the thing of the future.

209
00:13:08,540 --> 00:13:12,140
Automobile registrations, which numbered 8,000 nationwide

210
00:13:12,240 --> 00:13:17,100
in 1900, skyrocketed to 900,000 12 years later.

211
00:13:17,200 --> 00:13:20,620
The market for gasoline was suddenly enormous,

212
00:13:20,720 --> 00:13:23,220
and new supplies of oil were desperately needed

213
00:13:23,221 --> 00:13:26,080
to fill the tanks of these new tin lizzies.

214
00:13:27,140 --> 00:13:31,520
New oil fields were discovered in West Virginia, Ohio, and Indiana.

215
00:13:32,420 --> 00:13:36,460
Near the turn of the century, a large field was discovered in Southern California,

216
00:13:36,461 --> 00:13:40,500
and by 1910, California led the world in oil production,

217
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:44,220
most of it controlled by the Union Oil Company.

218
00:13:45,120 --> 00:13:49,840
And the oil fields became a backdrop for the new movie industry developing in Hollywood.

219
00:13:49,841 --> 00:13:55,840
But all this oil still wasn't enough to quench the insatiable thirst of the automobile.

220
00:13:55,841 --> 00:14:02,840
What the world needed was a huge new source of oil, and the man who would give it to them was Petillo Higgins.

221
00:14:02,841 --> 00:14:10,840
A one-armed lumberman from Beaumont, Texas, Higgins was fascinated by an area outside Beaumont known as Big Hill,

222
00:14:10,841 --> 00:14:14,840
where sulfur water and gas seeped up through the soil.

223
00:14:14,841 --> 00:14:18,840
Higgins believed there was oil to be found there, too.

224
00:14:18,841 --> 00:14:20,880
He just had an idea.

225
00:14:20,980 --> 00:14:22,640
It was a hunch.

226
00:14:22,740 --> 00:14:25,480
Let's call it divine if you want to.

227
00:14:25,580 --> 00:14:28,620
In fact, in the early days, more oil and gas

228
00:14:28,720 --> 00:14:32,080
was found by people drilling by the seat of the plants

229
00:14:32,180 --> 00:14:35,020
in the world by geology and geophysics.

230
00:14:35,120 --> 00:14:36,320
BILL KURTIS, JR.: Till that time,

231
00:14:36,420 --> 00:14:39,300
the oil that had been tapped was found between the Earth's

232
00:14:39,400 --> 00:14:43,360
surface and the first thick layer of underground rock.

233
00:14:43,460 --> 00:14:46,960
But Higgins believed there could be oil underneath that rock,

234
00:14:46,961 --> 00:14:50,160
that rock, oil under great pressure,

235
00:14:50,161 --> 00:14:54,460
great enough to force up the gases Big Hill was known for.

236
00:14:54,461 --> 00:14:56,660
The geologists he brought to the site

237
00:14:56,661 --> 00:15:00,380
all dismissed Big Hill as oil field material.

238
00:15:00,381 --> 00:15:03,320
And in seven years of trying to prove them wrong,

239
00:15:03,321 --> 00:15:08,040
Higgins had nothing but three dry holes to show for it.

240
00:15:08,041 --> 00:15:12,680
They put an ad in the paper about he wanted to drill this well,

241
00:15:12,681 --> 00:15:15,080
where it was, and everything else.

242
00:15:15,081 --> 00:15:20,200
And he only got one reply, and that was from Anthony Lucas.

243
00:15:21,480 --> 00:15:26,360
Anthony Lucas was a mining engineer and former captain in the Austrian Navy,

244
00:15:26,560 --> 00:15:28,520
who thought Higgins might be right.

245
00:15:28,980 --> 00:15:33,280
He formed a group of investors, giving Higgins 10% as a finder's fee,

246
00:15:33,281 --> 00:15:39,640
and in October of 1900 began drilling in an area of the hill known as Spindletop Heights.

247
00:15:39,641 --> 00:15:46,420
The type of drilling system Lucas and Higgins used was known as a rotary rig and is essentially

248
00:15:46,421 --> 00:15:50,300
the same drilling system used to find oil today.

249
00:15:50,301 --> 00:15:55,200
This derrick is used to support the weight of the drilling pipe.

250
00:15:55,201 --> 00:16:01,360
As you drill, you're coming in and out of the hole and you need a huge block and tackle,

251
00:16:01,361 --> 00:16:06,280
essentially what it is, that will pull this pipe out and pull up new joints to screw in

252
00:16:06,281 --> 00:16:07,920
to make deeper hole.

253
00:16:07,921 --> 00:16:14,080
So you have to have a very, very strong derrick to support that way.

254
00:16:14,081 --> 00:16:17,700
When they're drilling these wells, they have a bit on the end of the pipe.

255
00:16:17,701 --> 00:16:19,880
And they start to hold and go down.

256
00:16:19,881 --> 00:16:25,680
And every time they want to go deeper, they just screw on another 30 foot of drill pipe.

257
00:16:25,681 --> 00:16:27,420
And they just keep turning to the right.

258
00:16:27,421 --> 00:16:31,700
And the mud goes down the center of the pipe, goes through the bit, and out to outside in

259
00:16:31,701 --> 00:16:33,540
between it and where it has cut the hole.

260
00:16:33,541 --> 00:16:37,720
So it will take out the cutting.

261
00:16:37,721 --> 00:16:39,820
a steam-powered drill and crew of four,

262
00:16:39,821 --> 00:16:43,100
Lucas hit solid rock at 900 feet, the rock

263
00:16:43,101 --> 00:16:46,420
Petillo Higgins believed sat on top of the oil.

264
00:16:46,421 --> 00:16:49,560
With new drill bits designed for boring through rock,

265
00:16:49,561 --> 00:16:55,140
Lucas pushed on past 1,000 feet, then 1,100 feet.

266
00:16:55,141 --> 00:16:59,340
On January 10, 1901, at 1,160 feet,

267
00:16:59,341 --> 00:17:01,340
the bit broke through the rock.

268
00:17:01,341 --> 00:17:04,740
Suddenly, the well began spitting up mud, then lengths

269
00:17:04,741 --> 00:17:08,200
of the drill pipe itself, plus gas and rocks.

270
00:17:08,201 --> 00:17:12,660
And then, finally, oil, too, began shooting up into the sky

271
00:17:12,661 --> 00:17:17,060
with tremendous force, 150 feet high.

272
00:17:17,061 --> 00:17:18,680
It was pandemonium.

273
00:17:18,681 --> 00:17:20,860
Everybody went crazy.

274
00:17:20,861 --> 00:17:25,780
Lucas was in his home, and he was, he,

275
00:17:25,781 --> 00:17:27,980
somebody came running up to the house and said,

276
00:17:27,981 --> 00:17:30,420
it's in, it's in, it's in.

277
00:17:30,421 --> 00:17:35,320
And there was a man that was working in the rice fields

278
00:17:35,321 --> 00:17:36,021
nearby.

279
00:17:40,120 --> 00:17:45,860
And he turned around to his fellow worker,

280
00:17:45,861 --> 00:17:51,440
and he said, my god, what a gusher.

281
00:17:51,441 --> 00:17:56,600
And that was the first time the word gusher was applied

282
00:17:56,601 --> 00:18:00,260
to that well or any other well.

283
00:18:00,261 --> 00:18:06,340
It took the drill team a while to understand what was happening because there had never been a gusher before.

284
00:18:07,160 --> 00:18:11,540
In previous wells, oil had always flowed meekly or been pumped to the surface.

285
00:18:11,900 --> 00:18:18,660
But this was oil under pressure, immense pressure, just the way Petillo Higgins envisioned it.

286
00:18:18,661 --> 00:18:25,660
There had been some relatively big wells in Pennsylvania, but big wells usually meant 5,000 barrels a day.

287
00:18:25,661 --> 00:18:30,620
A spindle top came in massively on January 10th of 1901.

288
00:18:30,820 --> 00:18:35,360
The amount of oil produced that first day is usually stated at about 100,000 barrels.

289
00:18:36,200 --> 00:18:43,080
Within two days of the strike, more than 10,000 people came to Beaumont to see the largest oil strike in history

290
00:18:43,081 --> 00:18:47,140
and to launch a land and oil boom that put Titusville to shame.

291
00:18:48,380 --> 00:18:54,860
Four acres of land that sold for $60 one month were resold for $100,000 the next.

292
00:18:55,400 --> 00:18:56,920
It was hard not to get rich.

293
00:18:57,580 --> 00:19:01,440
By year's end, there were nearly 500 more gushers on Big Hill.

294
00:19:02,100 --> 00:19:07,820
Lucas sold out for $400,000 and 1,000 shares in the company his investors formed,

295
00:19:08,140 --> 00:19:12,240
which went on to become Gulf Oil and made Lucas extremely wealthy.

296
00:19:13,420 --> 00:19:15,880
Petillo Higgins had to sue to get his share

297
00:19:15,881 --> 00:19:19,940
and ended up accepting a settlement that brought him more than a million dollars.

298
00:19:19,941 --> 00:19:23,260
But he lost most of it the same way he earned it,

299
00:19:23,261 --> 00:19:26,860
searching for the next big strike.

300
00:19:26,960 --> 00:19:29,960
Petillo Higgins' great problem was that he had an imagination

301
00:19:30,060 --> 00:19:33,900
that was much vaster than his ability to control money.

302
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
He really was a visionary.

303
00:19:36,100 --> 00:19:38,900
He was a kind of romantic, loose in American business.

304
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:40,820
In a sense, he needed a bean counter.

305
00:19:40,920 --> 00:19:43,420
And had he had a bean counter, Petillo Higgins

306
00:19:43,520 --> 00:19:45,520
might have died rich.

307
00:19:45,620 --> 00:19:50,220
But when he died in 1955, Petillo Higgins wasn't bitter.

308
00:19:50,221 --> 00:19:52,860
Even though his money hadn't lasted long,

309
00:19:52,960 --> 00:19:55,620
he knew his name would live on forever.

310
00:19:55,720 --> 00:19:58,960
He had put Texas on the oil map.

311
00:19:59,060 --> 00:20:02,840
Petillo Higgins' Texas gusher sprayed 50,000 barrels of oil

312
00:20:02,940 --> 00:20:07,600
a day for nine days before the well could be controlled.

313
00:20:07,700 --> 00:20:10,600
In 1914, the world went to war.

314
00:20:10,700 --> 00:20:12,780
At first, it was fought in much the same way

315
00:20:12,880 --> 00:20:16,320
all previous wars were, by infantry troops who

316
00:20:16,420 --> 00:20:17,980
marched to battle.

317
00:20:17,981 --> 00:20:23,880
But by the time the United States entered the war in 1917, the face of war was changing.

318
00:20:24,460 --> 00:20:27,520
The biggest change of all was brought about by oil.

319
00:20:28,380 --> 00:20:31,700
The world's first tanks took to battle in 1916.

320
00:20:32,300 --> 00:20:36,140
Trucks took over from trains as the primary mode of troop transportation.

321
00:20:36,800 --> 00:20:43,320
And the war pushed the development of the airplane from a mere object of curiosity to the forefront of technology.

322
00:20:43,321 --> 00:20:46,282
In its year and a half in the war, the U.S.

323
00:20:46,283 --> 00:20:49,520
sent 50,000 gasoline-powered vehicles to France

324
00:20:49,521 --> 00:20:52,280
and produced 15,000 planes.

325
00:20:52,860 --> 00:20:56,260
When the war ended, Britain's Lord Curzon declared,

326
00:20:57,260 --> 00:21:01,060
The Allied cause had floated to victory upon a wave of oil.

327
00:21:02,180 --> 00:21:08,700
American oil and hardly any other made up that wave of petroleum on which the war was won.

328
00:21:08,701 --> 00:21:12,840
After the war, demand for gasoline and thus for oil

329
00:21:12,940 --> 00:21:15,240
continued to surge, and production

330
00:21:15,340 --> 00:21:17,440
barely kept up with demand.

331
00:21:17,540 --> 00:21:19,780
New sources were desperately needed,

332
00:21:19,880 --> 00:21:23,320
and once again, one of the oil industry's most unlike characters

333
00:21:23,420 --> 00:21:27,820
would step forward to deliver it, not with just another stripe,

334
00:21:27,920 --> 00:21:30,460
but with the biggest of them all.

335
00:21:30,560 --> 00:21:34,500
His name was Dad Joyner, a 70-year-old Texas oil

336
00:21:34,600 --> 00:21:38,700
man who had nothing going for him but nerve and luck.

337
00:21:38,701 --> 00:21:40,740
and most of that bad.

338
00:21:40,840 --> 00:21:44,780
With little income after years of one dry hole after another,

339
00:21:44,880 --> 00:21:48,640
Dad mostly lived off the kindness of well-to-do widows,

340
00:21:48,740 --> 00:21:52,320
whom he cultivated throughout Texas, said Dad.

341
00:21:52,420 --> 00:21:54,920
Well, every woman has a certain place on her neck,

342
00:21:55,020 --> 00:21:57,980
you see, and when I touch it, they automatically

343
00:21:58,080 --> 00:22:00,080
start writing me a check.

344
00:22:00,180 --> 00:22:01,820
He was a good man.

345
00:22:01,920 --> 00:22:03,220
Really, he was.

346
00:22:03,320 --> 00:22:06,920
He might have, he might have romanced all of them.

347
00:22:06,921 --> 00:22:11,600
And at 72 years old, I give him credit for doing whatever he could.

348
00:22:11,860 --> 00:22:14,860
He was a promoter, let's face it.

349
00:22:15,260 --> 00:22:20,220
And he would promote enough above the cost of the well to always keep some in his pocket.

350
00:22:20,980 --> 00:22:26,640
One of Joyner's widows, Daisy Bradford, owned a farm in Rusk County in East Texas

351
00:22:26,641 --> 00:22:28,760
that Dad thought might hold oil.

352
00:22:29,280 --> 00:22:32,120
And in 1927, he began drilling for it.

353
00:22:32,121 --> 00:22:36,860
Geologists and oil experts told Dad there was no chance oil

354
00:22:36,960 --> 00:22:38,260
would be found there.

355
00:22:38,360 --> 00:22:40,360
But like Petillo Higgins before him,

356
00:22:40,460 --> 00:22:43,860
Dad just had a good feeling about the site.

357
00:22:43,960 --> 00:22:46,840
Often paying his workers with dubious shares

358
00:22:46,940 --> 00:22:49,980
in future profits, he drilled for three years

359
00:22:50,080 --> 00:22:52,680
using beat up and borrowed equipment.

360
00:22:52,780 --> 00:22:56,420
In September 1930, there was a sign that Dad's luck might

361
00:22:56,520 --> 00:22:58,280
finally be changing.

362
00:22:58,281 --> 00:23:03,580
At 3,500 feet, the crew hit sand that seemed to have oil in it.

363
00:23:03,581 --> 00:23:08,180
A month later on October 3rd, a gurgling sound could be heard from the well.

364
00:23:08,181 --> 00:23:10,780
The drilling crew chief shouted to the crowd,

365
00:23:10,781 --> 00:23:12,780
Put out your cigarettes, quick!

366
00:23:12,781 --> 00:23:16,780
With a roar, oil began spouting over the top of the derrick.

367
00:23:16,781 --> 00:23:20,780
Dad Joiner had finally found black gold.

368
00:23:20,781 --> 00:23:24,280
Not only that, it was a spectacular find.

369
00:23:24,281 --> 00:23:29,980
an oil field that proved to be 45 miles long and 5 to 10 miles wide.

370
00:23:29,981 --> 00:23:34,440
It dwarfed Titusville, Spindletop, and Southern California put together.

371
00:23:35,440 --> 00:23:42,320
The field turned out to be, well, proportionate to a significant part of the whole state of Connecticut in the end.

372
00:23:42,780 --> 00:23:46,260
Truly massive in extent and also in production.

373
00:23:47,220 --> 00:23:51,680
It has produced today close to 7 billion barrels of oil.

374
00:23:54,280 --> 00:23:56,880
There was nothing like it.

375
00:23:56,980 --> 00:23:59,280
It was the largest discovery at that time

376
00:23:59,380 --> 00:24:03,080
in the entire Western Hemisphere.

377
00:24:03,180 --> 00:24:06,020
Seven months after Dad hit it big,

378
00:24:06,120 --> 00:24:08,720
1,000 wells had already been drilled,

379
00:24:08,820 --> 00:24:12,740
and the East Texas oil field, now known as the Black Giant,

380
00:24:12,840 --> 00:24:16,040
was producing 500,000 barrels a day,

381
00:24:16,140 --> 00:24:21,040
the equivalent of enough gasoline to drive to Jupiter.

382
00:24:21,041 --> 00:24:25,040
Dad Joiner made $1.5 million on his find,

383
00:24:25,041 --> 00:24:29,040
and in true-to-fore manner, he spent every penny of it

384
00:24:29,041 --> 00:24:32,040
searching vainly for another black giant,

385
00:24:32,041 --> 00:24:35,040
and romancing a long string of young women.

386
00:24:35,041 --> 00:24:39,040
When he died at age 87, he was living on donations

387
00:24:39,041 --> 00:24:42,040
from others he had helped make rich.

388
00:24:46,040 --> 00:24:49,040
The East Texas strike-jolted geologists

389
00:24:49,041 --> 00:24:51,780
out of their complacent belief that finding oil

390
00:24:51,880 --> 00:24:53,780
was simply a matter of identifying

391
00:24:53,880 --> 00:24:56,140
the correct surface features.

392
00:24:56,240 --> 00:24:58,620
And it ushered in a new era of advances,

393
00:24:58,720 --> 00:25:01,560
many of which came directly from new technology

394
00:25:01,660 --> 00:25:04,260
developed during World War I.

395
00:25:04,360 --> 00:25:08,760
The two most important were the airplane and the seismograph.

396
00:25:08,860 --> 00:25:10,660
With the airplane, the geologists

397
00:25:10,760 --> 00:25:13,160
took in a much broader perspective of surface

398
00:25:13,260 --> 00:25:17,060
geology than they could get from the ground.

399
00:25:17,061 --> 00:25:20,940
Photogeology really is what it was and they would use it and then they would send

400
00:25:20,941 --> 00:25:25,740
geologists on the ground to look at these areas. So aerial photography has

401
00:25:25,741 --> 00:25:30,020
been important in the oil industry. I don't think it's it's used now in

402
00:25:30,021 --> 00:25:36,560
satellite photography that they're using it worldwide to look at you know the

403
00:25:36,561 --> 00:25:41,120
big picture. The seismograph which measured shock waves passing through the

404
00:25:41,121 --> 00:25:45,200
earth and was originally developed to monitor earthquakes was used during

405
00:25:45,201 --> 00:25:50,000
during World War I to pinpoint enemy artillery batteries.

406
00:25:50,001 --> 00:25:52,040
After the war, geologists discovered

407
00:25:52,041 --> 00:25:54,200
they could set off explosive charges

408
00:25:54,201 --> 00:25:57,440
and record the energy waves that bounced back

409
00:25:57,441 --> 00:25:59,380
on the seismograph.

410
00:25:59,381 --> 00:26:00,980
The form of these waves helped them

411
00:26:00,981 --> 00:26:05,080
plot the shape and depth of underground formations.

412
00:26:05,081 --> 00:26:08,020
Just as oil had powered the war, the innovations

413
00:26:08,021 --> 00:26:11,420
of the battlefield were now helping produce more oil.

414
00:26:11,421 --> 00:26:13,900
But as the 1940s approached, the pendulum

415
00:26:13,901 --> 00:26:16,940
about to swing back again to war.

416
00:26:17,040 --> 00:26:22,380
And whoever controlled the oil could control the battlefield.

417
00:26:22,480 --> 00:26:24,240
John Wilkes Booth and two partners

418
00:26:24,340 --> 00:26:28,120
formed the Dramatic Oil Company to drill for oil.

419
00:26:28,220 --> 00:26:30,420
Six weeks after Booth shot President Lincoln,

420
00:26:30,520 --> 00:26:32,920
his well struck oil.

421
00:26:33,020 --> 00:26:35,080
World War II was a mechanized war,

422
00:26:35,180 --> 00:26:37,520
the likes of which had never been seen.

423
00:26:37,620 --> 00:26:39,760
Armies no longer moved on their stomachs.

424
00:26:39,860 --> 00:26:43,600
They moved in huge fleets of tanks, trucks, jeeps,

425
00:26:43,601 --> 00:26:48,040
ships, and planes, and keeping them moving required oceans

426
00:26:48,041 --> 00:26:52,700
of gasoline and oil, most of it from Dad Joiner's East Texas

427
00:26:52,701 --> 00:26:53,880
Black Giant.

428
00:26:53,881 --> 00:26:57,140
The Battle of the Atlantic is being fought with oil

429
00:26:57,141 --> 00:26:59,380
and will be won by oil.

430
00:26:59,381 --> 00:27:01,660
But a serious problem was getting that oil

431
00:27:01,661 --> 00:27:04,860
from the Lone Star State to the industrialized East,

432
00:27:04,861 --> 00:27:08,000
where the major war plants and shipping yards were,

433
00:27:08,001 --> 00:27:11,500
and to the European theater itself.

434
00:27:11,501 --> 00:27:18,460
The great problem that the United States had in 1940-41 was that with the beginning of the European War,

435
00:27:18,920 --> 00:27:23,700
Germany began submarine warfare in the area off the Texas Gulf.

436
00:27:24,420 --> 00:27:26,920
And they did destroy a number of tankers.

437
00:27:27,040 --> 00:27:33,960
They made it very risky and very dangerous to try to ship very, very much crude oil or finished product in that particular way.

438
00:27:34,460 --> 00:27:39,960
Extra trains went to work running tanker cars round the clock to keep the oil flowing.

439
00:27:39,961 --> 00:27:41,960
But it still wasn't enough.

440
00:27:42,060 --> 00:27:44,600
With the fate of the war effort hanging in the balance,

441
00:27:44,700 --> 00:27:47,540
the nation's oil men made a daring proposal.

442
00:27:47,640 --> 00:27:50,300
They wanted to build the biggest and longest pipeline

443
00:27:50,400 --> 00:27:53,700
on the face of the Earth, two feet in diameter,

444
00:27:53,800 --> 00:27:56,040
large enough for a man to crawl through.

445
00:27:56,140 --> 00:27:58,780
And running all the way from Houston to New York,

446
00:27:58,880 --> 00:28:02,040
they called it the Big Inch Pipeline.

447
00:28:02,140 --> 00:28:05,080
But the $70 million cost and the massive allotment

448
00:28:05,180 --> 00:28:08,180
of wartime steel made it a difficult proposal

449
00:28:08,181 --> 00:28:10,180
to sell in Washington.

450
00:28:10,181 --> 00:28:13,180
Steel was a strategic commodity at the time.

451
00:28:13,181 --> 00:28:16,180
If you look within the regulatory parts of the government,

452
00:28:16,181 --> 00:28:19,180
there was a kind of war going on internally in America

453
00:28:19,181 --> 00:28:22,180
between parts of the economy that wanted supplies and materials,

454
00:28:22,181 --> 00:28:25,180
and the people who wanted to promote Big Inch, Little Inch,

455
00:28:25,181 --> 00:28:28,180
were competing with the people who were building tanks and so on.

456
00:28:28,181 --> 00:28:31,180
It took a year, and the final and best argument

457
00:28:31,181 --> 00:28:34,180
came in the form of Nazi torpedoes.

458
00:28:34,181 --> 00:28:39,340
By early 1942, German subs were sinking an average of three tankers per day.

459
00:28:40,540 --> 00:28:46,880
In August 1942, an army of 15,000 workers began the Herculean task

460
00:28:46,881 --> 00:28:52,860
of building a 1,200-mile-long pipeline over rivers, swamps, and mountain ranges.

461
00:28:53,480 --> 00:28:56,920
It was a demanding task and on an impossible timetable.

462
00:28:57,620 --> 00:28:59,060
Routes were surveyed and cleared.

463
00:28:59,540 --> 00:29:02,420
Trenches three feet wide and four feet deep were dug.

464
00:29:02,421 --> 00:29:07,220
40-foot sections of pipe were welded together in the trenches and then covered.

465
00:29:07,221 --> 00:29:10,720
The work went on in even the harshest of winter weather.

466
00:29:11,700 --> 00:29:15,960
The pipeline was pushed north at record speed, almost four miles a day,

467
00:29:16,400 --> 00:29:20,260
from Houston to Illinois, then east to Philadelphia and New York.

468
00:29:20,920 --> 00:29:25,720
On New Year's Eve 1942, barely four months after construction began,

469
00:29:26,100 --> 00:29:28,640
the first oil was fed into the pipeline.

470
00:29:28,641 --> 00:29:35,040
So desperate was the need for oil that the last leg of the line hadn't even been completed yet.

471
00:29:35,041 --> 00:29:41,960
But it took three and a half days for the oil to travel the 1,254 mile length of the pipe.

472
00:29:41,961 --> 00:29:47,260
And by the time the first oil arrived, the last leg was in place and waiting.

473
00:29:48,320 --> 00:29:54,080
With so much of the country's oil output diverted to war, consumers at home faced shortages.

474
00:29:54,081 --> 00:29:57,080
Because of the necessity of diverting tankers

475
00:29:57,081 --> 00:30:01,080
to satisfy and fulfill the requirements of the Army and Navy,

476
00:30:01,081 --> 00:30:07,080
your government has found it necessary to ration gasoline

477
00:30:07,081 --> 00:30:09,080
on the eastern seaboard.

478
00:30:09,081 --> 00:30:12,080
But consumers' patience would be rewarded

479
00:30:12,081 --> 00:30:14,080
with not only an Allied victory,

480
00:30:14,081 --> 00:30:18,080
but with a revolution in post-war consumer goods.

481
00:30:18,081 --> 00:30:22,080
It would come from oil scientists whose contributions to the war

482
00:30:22,081 --> 00:30:25,780
to the war were as great as any soldiers.

483
00:30:25,880 --> 00:30:29,480
Much as gasoline was a byproduct of kerosene production,

484
00:30:29,580 --> 00:30:32,420
substances like ethane, propane, and benzene

485
00:30:32,520 --> 00:30:35,820
were byproducts of gasoline production.

486
00:30:35,920 --> 00:30:37,760
And in laboratories across the country,

487
00:30:37,860 --> 00:30:41,240
scientists experimenting with these new substances

488
00:30:41,340 --> 00:30:44,640
called petrochemicals turn them into new products,

489
00:30:44,740 --> 00:30:49,840
plastic, synthetic fibers, bonding cement.

490
00:30:49,841 --> 00:30:50,541
When U.S.

491
00:30:50,542 --> 00:30:57,000
bombers attacked Tokyo, their radar cables were shielded with a newly developed substance called polyethylene.

492
00:30:57,880 --> 00:31:04,860
When Allied paratroopers landed behind the lines at the beaches of Normandy, their parachutes were made of nylon.

493
00:31:05,560 --> 00:31:11,320
Probably the most well-known consumer products were nylon, Orlon, and all of the related kinds of fibers.

494
00:31:11,680 --> 00:31:17,900
To provide material that had very high tensile strength and very low weight, something that you couldn't do with cotton.

495
00:31:17,901 --> 00:31:25,900
You wouldn't really want to jump out of a military plane at a great height in a cotton parachute, for example, with cotton harnesses and so on.

496
00:31:26,440 --> 00:31:32,560
So there were really special operational problems that synthetic fibers ultimately addressed during the war.

497
00:31:32,880 --> 00:31:39,280
And after the war, turning these new products into consumer goods for a victorious, baby-booming new leisure class

498
00:31:39,281 --> 00:31:43,200
made the petrochemical industry the growth industry in America.

499
00:31:43,201 --> 00:31:50,740
In fact, from hula hoops to home carpeting, Americans embraced this new technology with vigor,

500
00:31:51,240 --> 00:31:57,300
and petrochemical sales averaged a 10% annual increase every year for well over two decades.

501
00:31:58,080 --> 00:32:01,680
The post-war baby boom meant millions of new mouths to feed,

502
00:32:02,020 --> 00:32:07,520
and oil-based fertilizers and pesticides made possible the agricultural advances to feed them.

503
00:32:07,521 --> 00:32:16,100
But all this, plus the new generation of gas-guzzling luxury cars, created yet another massive change in the oil industry.

504
00:32:16,880 --> 00:32:24,300
Demand so far outstripped supply that by the 1950s, America could no longer supply its oil needs itself.

505
00:32:31,400 --> 00:32:34,700
The new source for oil for the world was the Mideast.

506
00:32:34,701 --> 00:32:41,500
In 1938, a standard oil of California exploratory well in Saudi Arabia came in big.

507
00:32:41,920 --> 00:32:45,060
That same year, drillers hit a massive find in Kuwait.

508
00:32:46,160 --> 00:32:49,240
With post-war development of these enormous oil fields,

509
00:32:49,500 --> 00:32:53,120
vast quantities of cheap oil was soon pouring out of the Mideast.

510
00:32:53,460 --> 00:32:57,840
But getting it to America in the quantities now needed posed a major problem.

511
00:32:58,460 --> 00:33:01,700
The oil tankers of the time were simply too small.

512
00:33:01,701 --> 00:33:07,440
The first oil tanker was built in 1886 and had a capacity of 300 tons.

513
00:33:08,560 --> 00:33:14,300
Tanker size increased gradually to about 300 feet and 10,000 to 15,000 tons

514
00:33:14,301 --> 00:33:17,400
and stayed there until after World War II.

515
00:33:18,320 --> 00:33:21,860
But the post-war increases were nothing short of astounding.

516
00:33:22,400 --> 00:33:26,540
In 1950, the largest tankers in the world were 500 feet long

517
00:33:26,541 --> 00:33:29,040
and had a capacity of 25,000 tons.

518
00:33:29,041 --> 00:33:35,040
By 1975, they were 1,400 feet long, almost five football fields,

519
00:33:35,041 --> 00:33:38,040
with a capacity of 500,000 tons.

520
00:33:38,041 --> 00:33:43,040
The oil is held in numerous compartments designed to give the ship stability.

521
00:33:43,041 --> 00:33:48,040
They also have double hulls for protection against punctures in case of accidents.

522
00:33:50,040 --> 00:33:56,040
The tankers are fully computerized and operate with a crew of only 20 to 25,

523
00:33:56,041 --> 00:34:00,180
one-third the number of men Columbus needed on his tiny

524
00:34:00,280 --> 00:34:02,840
flagship, the Santa Maria.

525
00:34:02,940 --> 00:34:05,220
Today's tankers have grown so huge,

526
00:34:05,320 --> 00:34:07,760
they often can't even come to shore.

527
00:34:07,860 --> 00:34:10,780
There aren't piers big enough or harbors deep enough

528
00:34:10,880 --> 00:34:11,920
to hold them.

529
00:34:12,020 --> 00:34:14,520
They anchor offshore and disgorge their oil

530
00:34:14,620 --> 00:34:17,260
through flexible pipelines, either directly

531
00:34:17,360 --> 00:34:20,660
to land-based storage facilities or to smaller vessels,

532
00:34:20,760 --> 00:34:23,160
which can dock on shore.

533
00:34:23,161 --> 00:34:28,160
But since the 1980s, tanker production has slowed down somewhat.

534
00:34:28,260 --> 00:34:32,160
The OPEC oil embargo of the 70s and the resulting gas shortage

535
00:34:32,260 --> 00:34:37,200
in America launched a new emphasis on energy conservation.

536
00:34:37,300 --> 00:34:39,680
At the same time, the largest new field

537
00:34:39,780 --> 00:34:43,020
since Dad Joiner's Black Giant was discovered

538
00:34:43,120 --> 00:34:45,680
on the North Slope of Alaska.

539
00:34:45,780 --> 00:34:49,320
The building of the 800-mile-long Alaska pipeline,

540
00:34:49,321 --> 00:34:52,800
pipeline, some of it built on stilts to allow caribou

541
00:34:52,801 --> 00:34:56,100
to pass below it, brought massive new quantities

542
00:34:56,101 --> 00:34:59,760
of homegrown black gold into the American market.

543
00:34:59,761 --> 00:35:02,640
But even with the bounty of Alaska's Prudhoe Bay,

544
00:35:02,641 --> 00:35:06,240
oil men are still looking toward the sea for more oil,

545
00:35:06,241 --> 00:35:08,480
not for the next approaching tanker,

546
00:35:08,481 --> 00:35:13,380
but for a black harvest from the ocean floor itself.

547
00:35:13,381 --> 00:35:17,620
In California, Texas, Louisiana, major oil strikes

548
00:35:17,621 --> 00:35:21,580
were hit in coastal areas, and nearly everyone in the industry

549
00:35:21,581 --> 00:35:25,420
assumed there was oil to be found offshore.

550
00:35:25,421 --> 00:35:28,380
But until the late 1940s, no one really

551
00:35:28,381 --> 00:35:31,500
believed there was any way of getting it.

552
00:35:31,501 --> 00:35:35,300
As early as the 1890s, oil men in Southern California

553
00:35:35,301 --> 00:35:37,620
had drilled off short piers.

554
00:35:37,621 --> 00:35:41,200
But the idea of going farther out to sea was scoffed at,

555
00:35:41,201 --> 00:35:44,140
especially by the major oil companies.

556
00:35:44,141 --> 00:35:46,780
For them, there was still plenty of oil on land,

557
00:35:46,781 --> 00:35:50,700
and they weren't about to take a costly gamble looking for it offshore.

558
00:35:51,780 --> 00:35:55,480
And that was just fine with Robert Kerr and Dean McGee,

559
00:35:55,860 --> 00:35:59,120
partners in a small Oklahoma independent oil company.

560
00:35:59,700 --> 00:36:06,420
Started in 1929, Kerr-McGee needed an edge to compete with the already established major oil companies,

561
00:36:06,620 --> 00:36:08,160
so they looked to the sea.

562
00:36:08,480 --> 00:36:14,380
80% of all of the oil ever found in the world was found by independents, believe it or not.

563
00:36:14,381 --> 00:36:18,260
And we've drilled 80% of the wells.

564
00:36:19,180 --> 00:36:21,980
Major companies, they have their way of thinking,

565
00:36:23,200 --> 00:36:27,820
and they just felt like they didn't want to do anything unless it was proven.

566
00:36:28,400 --> 00:36:33,640
But Kerr-McGee was run by a geologist by the name of McGee.

567
00:36:34,440 --> 00:36:39,120
Dean McGee had an idea that oil would be found in the Gulf of Mexico.

568
00:36:39,121 --> 00:36:46,580
In 1945, Kerr-McGee began underwater seismic testing on the ocean floor in the Gulf.

569
00:36:47,160 --> 00:36:50,980
There was not a single piece of equipment designed for offshore drilling.

570
00:36:51,500 --> 00:36:57,700
So when they picked two likely sites, Kerr-McGee improvised the technology as they went along

571
00:36:57,701 --> 00:37:05,660
and built the world's first two offshore oil platforms in 18 feet of water, totally out of sight of land.

572
00:37:05,661 --> 00:37:12,260
Surplus vessels purchased from the Navy and anchored alongside served as crew quarters.

573
00:37:13,140 --> 00:37:17,320
Once the platforms were built, the drill heads were simply lowered into the water

574
00:37:17,321 --> 00:37:21,120
till they hit bottom and drilling through the ocean floor began.

575
00:37:22,440 --> 00:37:25,480
Two and a half weeks later, on one of the platforms,

576
00:37:25,740 --> 00:37:28,560
ten and a half miles off the coast of Louisiana,

577
00:37:29,180 --> 00:37:32,060
a crewman reported oil coming back with the mud.

578
00:37:32,061 --> 00:37:35,100
Misunderstanding, his boss told him to get a skimmer

579
00:37:35,200 --> 00:37:36,800
and skim it off.

580
00:37:36,900 --> 00:37:38,800
Skim it off hell, he replied.

581
00:37:38,900 --> 00:37:41,440
There's barrels of it.

582
00:37:41,540 --> 00:37:45,880
It was a beautiful Sunday morning, October 4, 1947.

583
00:37:45,980 --> 00:37:52,120
And suddenly, the oil industry had entire new oceans to conquer.

584
00:37:52,220 --> 00:37:55,520
Despite the fact that an offshore well cost five times

585
00:37:55,620 --> 00:37:58,480
more to drill than on land, within two years,

586
00:37:58,580 --> 00:38:02,060
there were already a score of platforms in the Gulf.

587
00:38:02,061 --> 00:38:04,400
And the new sea creatures were soon appearing

588
00:38:04,500 --> 00:38:10,560
on both the Pacific and Atlantic shores as well.

589
00:38:10,660 --> 00:38:15,000
By the mid-1960s, platforms were reaching as far as 30 miles

590
00:38:15,100 --> 00:38:19,780
out to sea, and by the 1970s, 100 miles.

591
00:38:19,880 --> 00:38:23,120
Today's platforms are true marvels of engineering.

592
00:38:23,220 --> 00:38:27,480
Crews on a single platform may drill 50 or more wells over time.

593
00:38:27,580 --> 00:38:30,780
More than 100 workers may live on the rigs, which

594
00:38:30,781 --> 00:38:33,280
which operate on round-the-clock schedules.

595
00:38:34,200 --> 00:38:38,520
The oil is usually piped directly to waiting tankers that moor nearby.

596
00:38:39,780 --> 00:38:43,540
The workers typically stay for two-week shifts at a time,

597
00:38:43,541 --> 00:38:47,540
and the rigs are designed to be completely self-contained housing units,

598
00:38:48,280 --> 00:38:52,400
featuring huge 24-hour kitchens, self-service laundromats,

599
00:38:52,600 --> 00:38:57,720
and even such landlubber amenities as movie theaters to break the monotony.

600
00:38:57,721 --> 00:39:04,860
And unlike those first Kerr-McGee platforms, oil men today aren't just drilling in shallow waters.

601
00:39:04,861 --> 00:39:14,660
When Shell Oil wanted to explore one of its offshore Gulf sites, it was faced with the problem of how to drill in water almost 1,400 feet deep.

602
00:39:15,860 --> 00:39:17,266
Their solution?

603
00:39:17,267 --> 00:39:26,920
The world's largest oil platform, 1,615 feet tall, 161 feet taller than the world's highest skyscraper,

604
00:39:26,921 --> 00:39:30,760
the Chicago Sears Tower.

605
00:39:30,860 --> 00:39:33,260
Not only was this an unheard of feat,

606
00:39:33,360 --> 00:39:36,360
the site 150 miles southwest of New Orleans

607
00:39:36,460 --> 00:39:39,300
was so inhospitable that the platform

608
00:39:39,400 --> 00:39:42,900
would have to be designed to withstand 140 mile an hour

609
00:39:43,000 --> 00:39:48,180
winds and hurricane waves of 70 feet.

610
00:39:48,280 --> 00:39:51,540
The project began on dry land in Corpus Christi when workers

611
00:39:51,640 --> 00:39:55,280
welded together 77,000 tons of steel

612
00:39:55,281 --> 00:40:00,260
to form the jacket, the legs and base structure the platform itself rests on.

613
00:40:00,900 --> 00:40:06,480
The leg supports alone are 10 feet in diameter, large enough to drive a car through.

614
00:40:07,180 --> 00:40:09,980
But building it on dry land was only the first step.

615
00:40:10,320 --> 00:40:13,440
The next problem was how to get it to the offshore site.

616
00:40:13,980 --> 00:40:19,080
It would have to be towed, and that meant they had to build the largest barge ever constructed

617
00:40:19,081 --> 00:40:22,520
with a deck area equivalent to an aircraft carrier,

618
00:40:22,521 --> 00:40:25,860
And even then, several hundred feet of the big rig stuck out

619
00:40:25,960 --> 00:40:28,420
over both ends of the barge.

620
00:40:28,520 --> 00:40:31,000
Once at the site, workers slipped the jacket,

621
00:40:31,100 --> 00:40:33,560
which was equipped with flotation devices,

622
00:40:33,660 --> 00:40:39,000
into the sea and slowly lowered it into position.

623
00:40:39,100 --> 00:40:43,840
It covered at the base 4 and 1⁄2 acres of ocean bottom.

624
00:40:43,940 --> 00:40:46,740
Huge underwater pile driving hammers

625
00:40:46,840 --> 00:40:51,820
then drove 28 piles into the seabed to anchor the platform.

626
00:40:51,821 --> 00:40:55,320
some reaching more than 400 feet into the ground.

627
00:40:56,220 --> 00:40:59,520
Engineers monitored the whole process through TV cameras

628
00:40:59,521 --> 00:41:02,160
mounted on remote-operated underwater vehicles.

629
00:41:03,500 --> 00:41:06,740
The deck area, 38,000 square feet of it,

630
00:41:07,040 --> 00:41:10,440
which was also built on land, was then fitted on top.

631
00:41:11,300 --> 00:41:14,440
The platform was designed to hold as many as 60 wells,

632
00:41:14,920 --> 00:41:18,580
reaching as deep as 18,000 feet below the ocean floor,

633
00:41:18,581 --> 00:41:22,120
with the yield of 44,000 barrels a day.

634
00:41:23,500 --> 00:41:25,840
Total cost? $500 million.

635
00:41:26,540 --> 00:41:33,400
And that doesn't include the $35 million shell paid the government to lease the underwater land.

636
00:41:34,620 --> 00:41:39,520
Today, more than one-third of the world's entire production of oil comes from the sea,

637
00:41:39,521 --> 00:41:43,780
a percentage that will continue to rise as technology makes it possible

638
00:41:43,781 --> 00:41:47,280
to go after undersea fields in even deeper water.

639
00:41:48,580 --> 00:41:53,480
Shell is drilling a well in the Gulf of Mexico and 2,800 and 60 feet of water.

640
00:41:57,620 --> 00:42:01,800
Technology will improve what we're going to be drilling in 5,000 feet of water.

641
00:42:02,280 --> 00:42:06,640
I won't live to see today, but I bet you they'll be drilling in 10,000 feet of water.

642
00:42:06,641 --> 00:42:20,641
In the 140 years since Edwin Drake discovered the greatest source of energy the world has ever

643
00:42:22,560 --> 00:42:26,008
known, the oil industry has changed immensely.

644
00:42:26,009 --> 00:42:29,680
But the men who make up that industry have hardly

645
00:42:29,681 --> 00:42:36,400
changed at all. The lure of oil has always drawn a special breed of adventurer, part

646
00:42:36,401 --> 00:42:42,777
businessman, part riverboat gambler, part scientist, part snake oil salesman.

647
00:42:42,778 --> 00:42:43,478
But with

648
00:42:43,479 --> 00:42:49,620
an indomitable spirit and a doggedness to pursue the siren song of black gold wherever

649
00:42:49,621 --> 00:42:50,620
it is heard.

650
00:42:50,621 --> 00:42:59,360
As long as there is a need for petroleum, and as long as it is possible to hunt for

651
00:42:59,361 --> 00:43:04,060
It will not only be searched for, but it will be produced.

652
00:43:04,160 --> 00:43:08,340
And irrespective of where it is found,

653
00:43:08,440 --> 00:43:13,700
it will be brought to market if it is needed.

654
00:43:13,800 --> 00:43:16,880
It isn't just the money that comes with oil,

655
00:43:16,980 --> 00:43:19,420
although the degree of riches some have found

656
00:43:19,520 --> 00:43:22,020
is almost unimaginable.

657
00:43:22,120 --> 00:43:24,060
But there's more to it than that.

658
00:43:24,160 --> 00:43:28,060
It has to do with sinking a well into the unknown,

659
00:43:28,061 --> 00:43:31,900
Making a financial and emotional gamble that comes up empty

660
00:43:32,000 --> 00:43:37,400
time after time, chasing the elusive till one day you find

661
00:43:37,500 --> 00:43:38,900
it.

662
00:43:39,000 --> 00:43:41,540
And the thrill of that one single moment

663
00:43:41,640 --> 00:43:45,440
is what will always keep the search for oil alive.

664
00:43:45,540 --> 00:43:48,480
I've been right there many times.

665
00:43:48,580 --> 00:43:52,280
And I've seen when we take a core for the first time,

666
00:43:52,380 --> 00:43:54,720
and you pull it out, and you know you got a well,

667
00:43:54,820 --> 00:43:58,060
and you smell it, and you push it into your face,

668
00:43:58,061 --> 00:44:03,729
that's nothing. Nothing will beat that. Nothing.

669
00:44:03,730 --> 00:44:09,280
It's the most exhilarating, fantastic emotional

670
00:44:09,281 --> 00:44:10,560
feeling you can possibly get.

